<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Oil Rivers Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1884 CE – 1893 CE · capital Calabar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate over the trading states of the Niger Delta, named for the palm oil that flowed through their creeks to Liverpool.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>British consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-hewett">Edward Hewett</a> signed protection treaties with rulers of the delta states in 1884, ahead of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a>, and in June 1885 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> proclaimed the Oil Rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>. It covered the coast from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lagos-colony">Lagos Colony</a> to the Rio del Rey, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bonny-kingdom">Bonny Kingdom</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalabari-kingdom">Kalabari Kingdom</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nembe-kingdom">Nembe Kingdom</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-calabar">Old Calabar</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-opobo">Kingdom of Opobo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For several years British authority was exercised by a consul with few staff, relying on gunboats and on the cooperation of the delta's trading houses. British firms used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> to break the middlemen's hold on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> trade. In 1887 consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a> deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaja-of-opobo">King Jaja</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-opobo">Opobo</a>, and from 1891 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-maxwell-macdonald">Claude MacDonald</a>, as commissioner and consul-general, set up a proper administration at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-calabar">Old Calabar</a> with customs and courts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1893 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> was extended inland and renamed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a>, and in 1900 it became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a>. The old delta trading states survived as local authorities under British rule, their kings and house heads reduced to chiefs.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/oil-rivers-protectorate.jpg" alt="Oil Rivers Protectorate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Niger_Coast_Protectorate_(1893%E2%80%931899).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1884 CE – 1893 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Calabar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Edward Hewett, Harry Johnston, Claude MacDonald</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Ijaw, Efik, Igbo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oil Rivers</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1888&amp;focus=oil-rivers-protectorate" aria-label="Oil Rivers Protectorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4855 1987 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Oil Rivers Protectorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5175 2243L5159 2227M5175 2243L5199 2268M5175 2243L5209 2265M5175 2243L5193 2263M5175 2243L5178 2264M5175 2243L5231 2248M5175 2243L4997 651M5175 2243L5175 2243"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5159 2227h0M5199 2268h0M5209 2265h0M5193 2263h0M5178 2264h0M5231 2248h0M4997 651h0M5175 2243h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5175